==English==

===Etymology===
1650s, variant of {{m|en|rouse}},<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> possibly influenced by {{m|en|rout}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹaʊst/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-roust.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊst|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[rout]] out of [[bed]]; to [[rouse]]
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|authorlink=Mark Twain|first=Mark|last=Twain|title={{w|The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn}}|chapter=VII|passage="Why didn't you '''roust''' me out?" / "Well, I tried to, but I couldn't; I couldn't budge you." / "Well, all right. Don't stand there palavering all day, but out with you and see if there's a fish on the lines for breakfast. I'll be along in a minute."}}
#* '''1977-1980''', [[w:Lou Sullivan|Lou Sullivan]], personal diary, quoted in '''2019''', Ellis Martin, Zach Ozma (editors), ''We Both Laughed In Pleasure''
#*: Yesterday morn at 6:30 they '''rousted''' me '''up''' & gave me 2 shots in the behind.
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[harass]], to treat in a [[rough]] way.
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1962|title={{w|Cape Fear (1962 film)|Cape Fear}}|time=00:28:45|passage=My client is an ex-convict. He's been constantly harassed by the police... subjected to extreme mental cruelty and public degradation. He's even been denied an adequate place to live! To be very blunt, gentlemen, my client has been thoroughly '''rousted'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} to [[arrest]]
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to [[drive]] strongly {{rfex|en}} <!--what kind of drive?-->

